WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:08.940
SPEAKER: Good afternoon.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.866
Please welcome Oren Teich.

00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:16.289
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.340
OREN TEICH: Good afternoon, and
thank you for joining us here.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.270
It is an incredible
conference so far.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.070
I hope everyone's
really enjoying it.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.560
If you haven't had a
chance, I will strongly

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.500
encourage you to make sure you
find some time to check out all

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.870
of the different expo spaces.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.890
I happen to be a little bit
partial to the one downstairs.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.390
Directly below us
on the first floor

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.450
there's a really cool
air cannon you can fire.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:45.710
So my name is Oren Teich.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.820
I'm director of product
management at Google.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.190
I'm responsible for serverless.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.890
So obviously today we're going
to talk about serverless.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.910
I hope you guys like that stuff.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.190
I've only been at Google myself
for a little over a year.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.880
Been in the industry
for a long time,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.610
and really my whole time
been focused on what

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.020
we can do for developers.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.540
And part of why I'm so
excited to be here at Google

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.877
is the opportunity that it
gives us to not just offer you

00:01:11.877 --> 00:01:13.460
a single point
solution, but offer you

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.470
the whole comprehensive thing.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.620
Today I'm going to talk about
a lot of different components.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.730
And what I want you to
know is it is a lot.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.030
It is complicated.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.400
There's a lot of pieces.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.580
And what we're trying to do
is offer a very comprehensive

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.170
solution for our customers.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.530
And also keep in mind that
this is a point in time

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.370
that, you know, today is July
24, or whatever, of 2018.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.890
And there's going to be
August and September and July

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.660
24 of 2019.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:43.610
And we're constantly
iterating on this.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.110
And part of why I
bring this up is I

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.430
want to hear from you as well.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:47.870
I want to know the
feedback you have.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.190
Because we're trying to build
the best product for you.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.760
And so please, by all means,
come up, send me an email--

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.640
I'm just my last name--
at Twitter, or at Google,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.505
or whatever.

00:01:57.505 --> 00:01:59.380
But let us know how we
can make serverless as

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.249
successful for you as possible.

00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:04.040
So you know, I think
it's always important,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.110
because we're never
going to get over it,

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.289
to make fun of the word first.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:11.770
If I could choose any
other word in the world,

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.190
I wouldn't have the
word serverless.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.510
Clearly there are many, many
servers that are behind things.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.610
And in fact, no
surprise, you know,

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.100
Google is one of the biggest
server companies in the world,

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:22.600
right?

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.110
We make our own hardware.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.980
We have incredible data centers.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.950
And I think this is
really important,

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.490
because it's not just about the
physical machines, of course.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.562
It's about how you hook
them all up, what's

00:02:33.562 --> 00:02:35.645
involved in getting them
together, and maintaining

00:02:35.645 --> 00:02:36.615
and operating.

00:02:36.615 --> 00:02:38.990
And it's also about the people
that are behind it, right,

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.200
and how we actually
keep everything running.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.197
And in and of itself that's
important and exciting.

00:02:45.197 --> 00:02:46.280
But there's another piece.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.090
And I don't know if you
know this company Veolia.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.130
They're a huge French
multinational company.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.770
They used to be part of, I
think, the Vivendi group.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:01.340
Veolia has 350,000 employees.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.250
They take care of
trash, electricity,

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.910
a lot of municipal
services across Europe--

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.320
actually in 48 countries,
last I checked.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.800
And I was meeting with
their CIO earlier.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.320
And he said something that I
hadn't even considered before.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.720
And it's really struck me, and
making me think quite a bit.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.940
He was talking about
the importance of we

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.140
live in a scarce resource world.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.550
And the reality is that
as we move more and more

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.310
into computing, as we move and
take advantage of our compute

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.340
more and more, the
resource costs can go up.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.820
And his argument is our
moral responsibility

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.190
to use these efficiently,
that the only way

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.832
that we can be really
looking toward the future,

00:03:42.832 --> 00:03:44.790
and if software is eating
the world, having 10,

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.520
100, 1,000 times
more use of software

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.250
is if we don't use 10, 100,
1,000 times more resources

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:49.940
in the way.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.530
And their argument
is that serverless

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.586
is the way that they're
looking to do that.

00:03:54.586 --> 00:03:56.210
And I think that was
really insightful.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.168
I don't have any stats.

00:03:57.168 --> 00:03:58.340
I only heard this today.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.423
But it's something I'm
going to think about a lot,

00:04:00.423 --> 00:04:03.010
and go back and look into.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:08.330
So at the end of the day we
have these serverless products.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.340
And I think it's
like just recap why

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.080
are people we see choosing it?

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.270
And what we hear all
the time, right--

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.024
I mean, this is motherhood
and apple pie, right?

00:04:17.024 --> 00:04:18.649
What we hear all the
time is serverless

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.154
enhances dev productivity.

00:04:20.154 --> 00:04:21.820
It's about the
operational model, right?

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.990
And that last one-- pay
only for usage-- is often

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.645
the way it gets reduced down to.

00:04:25.645 --> 00:04:27.770
Of course, there's a fully
managed security, right?

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.230
We, of course, take care of
security patches and updates

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:31.604
for the system.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.770
Of course, you're not having
to think about servers.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.780
That is in the title.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.500
But this is just one piece
of the overall puzzle.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.210
And in fact, it's
one that I want

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.860
to expand upon a
little bit more,

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.290
because this is not sufficient
when you talk about it.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:49.525
Haha.

00:04:49.525 --> 00:04:51.439
I swatched slides
a few seconds ago.

00:04:51.439 --> 00:04:53.230
So we're going to talk
about why that's not

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:54.188
sufficient in a second.

00:04:54.188 --> 00:04:55.700
But before we do
that, I actually

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.045
want to get Deep up here.

00:04:57.045 --> 00:04:59.420
Deep is the executive director
from the "New York Times."

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.794
He's responsible for a lot of
the technical architecture.

00:05:01.794 --> 00:05:04.293
Deep, come on up and
join me on stage.

00:05:04.293 --> 00:05:08.141
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:09.152
DEEP KAPADIA: Hey, guys.

00:05:09.152 --> 00:05:11.610
OREN TEICH: So you know, I'm
not sure if everyone is aware.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.550
But "New York Times" has been
longtime customers of ours.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.040
They've been using App Engine.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.490
They're looking at a lot
of different products.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.406
Deep, maybe you could
give us a quick overview

00:05:19.406 --> 00:05:20.889
of how you use our products.

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:21.930
DEEP KAPADIA: Absolutely.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.910
So we decided to migrate
from our data centers

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.940
off to the public cloud
a couple of years ago.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:34.920
And Google seemed to have a very
compelling offering, especially

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.290
when it comes to
abstractions that

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.690
are available to developers.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:43.800
One of the big things was
the serverless platform,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.850
or what we knew as the
App Engine at the time.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.520
And, of course, the offering
has expanded to other things

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:51.330
at this point.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:54.510
But we started looking at
App Engine for workloads

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.512
that we wanted to
scale pretty fast.

00:05:57.512 --> 00:05:59.970
A lot of the traffic to the
"New York Times" is very spiky.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:06.390
The first thing that we looked
at was our crosswords product.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.780
Our crosswords is our
highest-grossing product

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.420
at this point.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:14.160
So it was a bit of a
chance that we took.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.370
But we looked at how
the scaling needs

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.230
worked for our crosswords app.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:23.640
And what we found was when
we published a crossword out,

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.400
people just have
at it right away.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.956
They want to solve the
crossword then and there.

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:31.830
And they're trying to
download the crossword,

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.340
and solve the puzzles
online, et cetera.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.940
And we just could not keep
up with the scaling needs.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.510
So we would just
over-provision infrastructure

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.240
for the crosswords app.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.360
And when we started looking
at App Engine, and its scaling

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.570
abilities, we found that
it was a perfect fit

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.880
for these spiky
workloads that we

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.000
have for the crosswords app--

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.570
and maybe for other things, too.

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:02.880
Oren and I were talking about
using it for our breaking news

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.650
alerts at some point, where we
could send out a breaking news

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:10.860
alert, and quickly ramp up
to the number of requests

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.900
that we would need to
serve at any given time.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.560
So that was one thing
that we looked at.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.550
So App Engine was
our first major foray

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:24.409
into application
development on GCP.

00:07:24.409 --> 00:07:25.950
Before that, we'd
been using BigQuery

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.160
for a little bit, which I've
always said was our gateway

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.450
drug into Google.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:32.210
But, yeah.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.110
OREN TEICH: And on
that, are you only using

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.130
App Engine and BigQuery?

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.046
How do you look at the
overall platform today?

00:07:37.046 --> 00:07:39.030
DEEP KAPADIA: So no we don't.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.760
We, in fact, use a
lot of Kubernetes.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.430
So we use GKE for workloads
that may not fit the App Engine

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.860
model in some ways
where we need to do

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:53.220
something very specific that
doesn't fit the App Engine

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:54.780
model.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:59.730
So we doubled down on
both App Engine and GKE

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:00.990
during the cloud migration.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:06.870
At this point, all of
newyorktimes.com runs on GKE.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.290
So that's another thing.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:10.950
But on the app engine side,
we have about 30 services

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.890
and applications that
run on App Engine.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.480
OREN TEICH: And it's not that
you just developed for it.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:17.970
You've also created some
incredible open-source

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.620
repositories around
it as well, right?

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:20.661
DEEP KAPADIA: Absolutely.

00:08:20.661 --> 00:08:24.150
And if you go to
github.com/nytimes,

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:27.210
there's a lot of open source
frameworks that are available

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.980
to people to go look at.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.766
And some of them also built
around App Engine as well.

00:08:33.766 --> 00:08:34.580
OREN TEICH: Cool.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.280
All right, well thank you Deep.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:36.230
I really appreciate it.

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:37.184
DEEP KAPADIA: Well,
thank you so much, Oren.

00:08:37.184 --> 00:08:37.559
OREN TEICH: All right.

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:38.361
Thanks.

00:08:38.361 --> 00:08:42.129
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:44.955 --> 00:08:47.810
Don't open the water.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.360
So obviously we have
other customers.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.210
"New York Times" is
just one of them.

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.229
Here's some others
that I really enjoyed

00:08:54.229 --> 00:08:55.270
hearing some quotes from.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.470
I'm not going to
read you the quotes.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.910
You can do that on your own.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:02.450
I will call out, in that upper
left corner, the Smart Parking

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.430
one.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:06.200
There's a fantastic session
that's coming up later held

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.340
by Morgan Hallmon.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.048
He's going to be
talking about doing more

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:10.520
with less in serverless events.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.080
And he's featuring
the architecture

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.980
of what Smart Parking has done.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.430
It's really, really
interesting to see

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:19.160
not just how people
are using serverless

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.590
for the operational
benefits, but also

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:22.994
for programming benefits.

00:09:22.994 --> 00:09:25.160
Because obviously, as you
decompose your application

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:28.280
into small pieces, as you move
into event-driven workloads,

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.780
you can start to
think about how you

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.880
do your computing differently.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.280
And Smart Parking is a
company that's built entirely

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.390
around this concept.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:39.350
And it's really remarkable
to see this collection of IoT

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.364
devices that are sending signals
out across an entire city,

00:09:42.364 --> 00:09:44.280
and how they aggregate
that for parking needs.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.480
So I'd strongly encourage you
to take a look at that session.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:51.110
And that's really a good segue
into even without understanding

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.500
the operational benefits
that we get, let's not

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:55.940
be fooling ourselves.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:58.024
Writing software is
still very, very hard.

00:09:58.024 --> 00:09:59.690
And something we hear
from our customers

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.769
all the time is
they come to Google

00:10:01.769 --> 00:10:04.310
because they don't want to be
in the infrastructure business.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:07.180
In fact, Nick, who is speaking
right now at same time--

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.180
Nick Rockwell, the CTO
of the "New York Times"--

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:11.580
we were having a meeting a
few months back in New York.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.860
And he just said flat out, "New
York Times" is a news business,

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.570
Google is the
infrastructure business.

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:19.970
And that's the core
value prop, of course,

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.577
of why people come
to us, and to GCP.

00:10:22.577 --> 00:10:24.410
But there's another
part of this too, right?

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:27.860
How can we help you not just
solve the infrastructure,

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.932
but solve the application
development problem?

00:10:29.932 --> 00:10:32.390
And I think this, to me, is
one of the most exciting pieces

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.940
about serverless, is it
gives us an opportunity

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.624
to revisit a lot of the core
things that were done before.

00:10:37.624 --> 00:10:40.290
Hey, maybe we don't need to have
IP-based security, for example.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.130
Maybe we can start to re-imagine
and re-architect things

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:44.990
for a more modern world.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.007
And so part of it
is how can we take

00:10:47.007 --> 00:10:49.340
all of these different pieces,
which historically you've

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.423
had to think about, right?

00:10:50.423 --> 00:10:52.310
Oh, what [INAUDIBLE]
solution do I use?

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:54.360
How am I going to
configure my networking?

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.020
All the pieces.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:59.780
And frankly, this slide
is on the lower end

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.040
of what the boxes look like.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.490
I've seen versions of this slide
which literally have 10, 20, 30

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:05.734
more boxes than this.

00:11:05.734 --> 00:11:07.400
And we want you to
just be able to focus

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:09.670
on building that application.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:12.171
And so I showed
this slide earlier.

00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:13.920
And we talked about
the operational model.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.010
And I would actually
say that serverless

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:17.166
is made up of two things.

00:11:17.166 --> 00:11:19.040
And I talk about this
all the time right now.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:20.240
There's the operational
model, which

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:21.560
I think we all understand.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.920
But equally important is
the programming model.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.920
And by the programming
model, I mean, of course,

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:27.350
that it's going to
be a service based.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.990
We can argue about the value of
monoliths versus microservices.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:31.800
And I'd be happy to do that.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:33.462
By the way, short answer.

00:11:33.462 --> 00:11:34.670
There is no one right answer.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.794
And chances are you should
start with the monolith.

00:11:36.794 --> 00:11:40.020
But anyway, still going to have
a services-based ecosystem,

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:40.520
right?

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.080
You're going to be using
things most likely,

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:43.860
like BigQuery or a
Redis, or a Memcache.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.280
You're going to have a whole
set of services around.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:48.470
Event-driven is incredibly
important in this, right?

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:50.428
And what I love about
event-driven architecture

00:11:50.428 --> 00:11:53.234
is you're shifting who has to
do a lot of the hard lifting.

00:11:53.234 --> 00:11:55.400
Instead of you having to
wire up all the components,

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:57.650
you let the infrastructure
provider-- us-- do it.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.245
And of course it has to be open.

00:11:59.245 --> 00:12:01.120
And we're going to talk
about this some more.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:03.536
You may have seen some of the
announcements that came out.

00:12:03.536 --> 00:12:06.740
But one of the
catches historically

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:08.900
has been if you buy into
your programming model,

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.640
you're stuck with that
programming model.

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.410
And we want to
make it possible so

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:17.060
that you can take advantage
of all these characteristics,

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:18.919
and do it anywhere you want.

00:12:18.919 --> 00:12:20.960
We think you're going to
do it on GCP, because we

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:22.936
think we have the best
operational model.

00:12:22.936 --> 00:12:24.810
But we're not going to
force you to be there.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.520
So we're going to talk about
that in quite some detail.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:29.480
Now, I talked about
services, right?

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.520
And someone-- I don't
remember-- a year ago

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.770
I saw on Twitter, someone
said that maybe we shouldn't

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:35.853
have called it serverless.

00:12:35.853 --> 00:12:37.640
We should have called
it "serviceful."

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.080
And I agree with that.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.630
Because of course
there's compute, right,

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:43.880
the ability to run some cycles.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.670
But the reality is it's all
of the services around it

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:47.960
that really make it useful.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:50.660
And just as a thought
exercise, how useful

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:52.610
is something if you
can't store any data

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:53.630
or have a cache, right?

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.060
It's awfully hard,
sometimes, to do anything

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.710
of any kind of scale there.

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:01.100
So all of these pieces, Google
has been in this business

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:01.790
for a long time.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:04.580
And we do have all
of these pieces.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:06.564
Given that I only
have 37 minutes left,

00:13:06.564 --> 00:13:08.480
I am not going to talk
about all these pieces,

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:09.620
as much as I'd love to.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.411
And there's been some
amazing announcements

00:13:11.411 --> 00:13:13.657
that we've had out there
today that we're not even

00:13:13.657 --> 00:13:14.490
going to talk about.

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:17.073
So, for example, in that lower
left corner of Cloud Datastore,

00:13:17.073 --> 00:13:19.430
there's a whole new product
called Cloud Firestore.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:20.600
It's incredibly scalable.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.900
It's built on a
high-scalability back-end.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:27.189
It has the same API as
available for Datastore.

00:13:27.189 --> 00:13:29.105
And now you get just a
bigger, better product.

00:13:29.105 --> 00:13:32.704
So that's something we announced
today, available for GCP.

00:13:32.704 --> 00:13:34.620
The list of things I'm
not going to talk about

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:36.200
is longer than what I
am going to talk about.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.480
But I want you to know
that they're there,

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:39.320
and encourage you to
do some more research.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:40.819
What we're going
to talk about today

00:13:40.819 --> 00:13:46.080
is the middle two
sections, the compute side.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.912
So first, App Engine.

00:13:47.912 --> 00:13:49.620
App Engine's been
around for a long time.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.730
I don't know if you know, it's
been around for over 10 years.

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:54.490
It's a really
remarkable product.

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:57.410
It predates Google Cloud.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.720
App Engine is the OG serverless.

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.310
It is really incredible product.

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:04.310
And I think if you're not
familiar with it, just

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:05.090
a quick recap.

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:07.125
It lets you take an app
source code, deploy it.

00:14:07.125 --> 00:14:08.750
And it's kind of
exactly what you want.

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:09.791
There's no configuration.

00:14:09.791 --> 00:14:12.380
You just type gcloud app
deploy, and away you go.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:13.340
It scales.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.570
Snapchat, famously, is on it.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:16.070
But of course Best
Buy, Pocket Gems.

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:17.900
You can see some other
customers using it.

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:21.635
And you're only paying for
your usage more or less.

00:14:21.635 --> 00:14:23.510
There's always small
caveats in these things.

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:24.650
It is old.

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:28.430
But that's not to say it isn't
without its imperfections.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.200
So one of the biggest
complaints that we

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.450
had from App Engine
for years is it had

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.380
a lot of proprietary pieces.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:37.180
So for example, if you
use the Java language,

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:38.740
there was class white-listing.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:41.225
There were only certain
allowed classes you could run.

00:14:41.225 --> 00:14:43.400
You couldn't take, if
you were in Python,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.890
you couldn't just take
open-source software from out

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:47.042
on the web and run it.

00:14:47.042 --> 00:14:48.500
And I don't know
if you've noticed,

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.161
open source is kind of a thing.

00:14:50.161 --> 00:14:52.160
It's worth noting 10 years
ago it wasn't, right?

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:54.500
When this came out, the
whole point of App Engine

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.840
is you're standing on
the shoulders of Google.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:58.430
But what's shifted
is now we need

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.990
you to be able to stand on the
shoulders of the community.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:02.750
And so we've been
thinking about this.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:08.090
And we've been limited because
of the security needs, right?

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:10.580
Because App Engine and the way
it runs in our data center,

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:12.950
we're letting anyone run
any arbitrary software.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:14.450
That's a huge
security risk for you.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.033
It's a huge security
risk for us if we

00:15:16.033 --> 00:15:17.210
don't manage that correctly.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:18.668
And so that's why
historically it's

00:15:18.668 --> 00:15:19.910
been very carefully managed.

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:23.480
But one thing Google definitely
has is some smart engineers.

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:25.940
And so for many years now
we've been working quietly

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:28.660
in the background on a
project that we announced

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:30.620
at KubeCon called gVisor.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:33.170
And gVisor was
specifically designed

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:34.610
to address this problem.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:38.840
And gVisor's an
open source project

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:39.800
as well that you can find out.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:41.000
We announced it at KubeCon.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.250
But gVisor is
designed to give you

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.394
the security you'd expect
from a virtual machine,

00:15:45.394 --> 00:15:47.060
but with the performance
characteristics

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:48.620
you'd expect from a container.

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:51.260
And so yay, it's cool.

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:53.900
It's implemented in user
space, so it's highly secure.

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:58.510
It's written in Go, because Go.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:01.140
But it's really been remarkable.

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:03.900
And what we do is we actually
implement the system calls,

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.995
intercept them, and
then, because we're

00:16:05.995 --> 00:16:07.370
intercepting them,
we can inspect

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:08.130
everything that's going on.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:09.210
We know exactly what's going on.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.490
We can make sure there's
no security issues.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:13.490
And because it's Go, we have
memory and typesafe constructs

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:14.490
in place.

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:18.680
So this is now what's
underpinning many products

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.440
at Google, especially all of
our serverless products moving

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:22.100
forward.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.680
And this is what's enabling
us to do everything

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:27.444
that I'm going to talk about
for the rest of this talk.

00:16:27.444 --> 00:16:28.860
So the first thing
it's enabled us

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:30.444
is with second-generation
run times.

00:16:30.444 --> 00:16:31.860
So the second-generation
run times

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.030
are all gVisor based for App
Engine-- sorry, second-gen app

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:36.670
engine run times.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:38.614
And so what we're
doing here is we're

00:16:38.614 --> 00:16:40.530
giving you an idiomatic
dev experience, right?

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.960
Historically you've had
to learn our YAML files.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.070
We want to bring it to
you, and have you do it.

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:46.320
You want to install a package?

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:47.610
What's the language say?

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:49.890
No more API restrictions,
like I mentioned.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:52.740
And frankly, it's just faster
if you just do benchmarking.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:57.840
And so I'm really excited
to announce that coming out

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.800
and rolling out in
the next 30 days,

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:04.040
we will have for App Engine
Standard Node.js 8, Python 3.7,

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:05.819
and PHP 7 all available.

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:08.284
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:08.284 --> 00:17:09.270
Thank you.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:20.310
This has been the
number one request

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:22.480
we've had literally for years.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.550
In fact, we, of course, have
our bug tracking system.

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:29.900
And the top of it has
been these languages.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:32.220
And people always ask,
why has it taken so long?

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:36.030
And the answer is we needed to
overhaul our entire security

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.340
infrastructure, this runtime
execution environment.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:39.840
But now that we've
done it, you can

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:42.000
see we're able to get a number
of languages in at once.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:43.749
And it means that we
can be more committed

00:17:43.749 --> 00:17:46.540
to being up to
date in the future.

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:48.979
So next I'd like to talk
about Cloud Functions.

00:17:48.979 --> 00:17:51.520
Hopefully you've all heard of
Cloud Functions already, right?

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:54.490
Cloud Functions lets you have
very small snippets of code.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.220
Again, it's going to
autoscale with usage.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:59.260
Really nicely, it
lets you pair them

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:01.844
to events, GCS storage
bucket, upload change.

00:18:01.844 --> 00:18:03.260
There's many, many
different event

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:04.660
writers you can hook it up to.

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:06.910
And, of course, you have
even finer-grained control

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:09.080
of where you're only
paying where code runs.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:11.870
So this has been a
long time coming.

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:13.480
We've been working
on Cloud Functions.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:17.660
Quite famously it's been
in beta now for too long.

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:21.760
So, thankfully, as of
today, it is fully GA.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:23.380
So I'm really excited.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:25.180
Cloud Functions is
now GA, and it's

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:27.340
available as of today
in four regions.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.230
You can get it in
East and Central,

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.390
as well as in Europe and Asia.

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:33.070
And uniquely in
the market, this is

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:36.044
covered by the same SLA as
the rest of Google products.

00:18:36.044 --> 00:18:37.960
People always ask, why
is this taking so long?

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.010
What was going on?

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:42.220
Not only did we need to
create this entirely new

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:43.855
infrastructure for
security sandboxing,

00:18:43.855 --> 00:18:45.480
but we also needed
to make sure that it

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:47.480
was going to work in the
reliability and quality

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:48.980
that our customers
are expecting.

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:51.867
And that-- I am embarrassed.

00:18:51.867 --> 00:18:53.950
It should be something we
were able to do quicker.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:55.292
But that investment takes time.

00:18:55.292 --> 00:18:57.250
And it's something that
we can now stand behind

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.020
and we're very proud
to make available.

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:00.980
So today, just go
sign up, use it.

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:01.690
It's available.

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:02.740
There's no sign-up list.

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:06.466
There's nothing you need to do.

00:19:06.466 --> 00:19:07.606
Now, that's, of--

00:19:07.606 --> 00:19:08.106
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:08.106 --> 00:19:08.606
Thanks.

00:19:11.557 --> 00:19:12.890
That's, of course, not all.

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:22.149
Having additional
languages, maybe we

00:19:22.149 --> 00:19:23.940
don't want the world
to be in a Node world.

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:27.060
I don't, but some might.

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.610
So obviously, having additional
languages is critical, too.

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:31.320
So this is coming
back to how we can

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:34.290
make the experience idiomatic
to what you and your company

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.010
wants.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:39.540
So GCF came out with Node 6.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.400
Again, rolling out
over the next 30 days,

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:44.610
we're going to have Node
8, as well as Python 3.7.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:48.285
And these are nice changes.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:56.000
One of my favorite books
is a book by Igor Gamov.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:59.680
He's the guy who came up
with the name the big bang.

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:00.817
And it's a book on math.

00:20:00.817 --> 00:20:02.775
And the title is just
"One Two Three Infinity."

00:20:02.775 --> 00:20:03.275
All right.

00:20:03.275 --> 00:20:05.519
And the point is you can
look at a sequence, right?

00:20:05.519 --> 00:20:07.060
Once you go from
one to two to three,

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:08.350
it's infinity and beyond.

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:10.529
So this would make
language two with Node 8

00:20:10.529 --> 00:20:11.820
and language three with Python.

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:14.200
So I look forward to
infinity coming soon as well.

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:15.658
But these are the
ones that we have

00:20:15.658 --> 00:20:16.950
available right now and today.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.116
Getting the infrastructure
in place to support these

00:20:19.116 --> 00:20:19.992
is great.

00:20:19.992 --> 00:20:22.200
Like I said, they're rolling
out in the next 30 days.

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:26.860
They'll be available to everyone
once the deploys are finished.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:28.350
Now that's not all, right?

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:31.060
Because, of course,
GCF is a new product.

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:33.700
It has a lot of new
capabilities that are necessary.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:36.630
So the first one, VPC and VPN.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:39.390
Something we hear all the time--
and Deep talked about this--

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:42.630
is we are not an island.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:45.484
No product inside of
Google is an island.

00:20:45.484 --> 00:20:47.400
What I hear time and
time again from customers

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:51.120
is customers come to us
for the platform of GCP.

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:53.550
And what's important is
that we work well together.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.560
And we can't do
everything from day one.

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:57.060
We have to iterate
our way there.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:59.640
But VPC and VPN are key
issues there, right?

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:02.700
So if you're not
familiar with VPC--

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:06.070
Virtual Private Controls-- and
the Virtual Private Network,

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:10.860
what this lets you do is it lets
you define a network that only,

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:12.870
for example, you
have your bridging

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.030
your on-prem into your cloud.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:17.980
You can control who
has access to it.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:19.140
This is super important.

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:20.460
And we've heard, for
example, from people

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:22.543
who might be running in
on-prem Cassandra cluster,

00:21:22.543 --> 00:21:24.720
and they want to trigger
a function execution.

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.850
With this new feature, you
will be able to do that.

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:28.350
This ties into the
next one as well,

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:29.610
which is security controls.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:32.010
Right now, if you deploy a
Cloud Function, it's public.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:33.030
It is on the internet.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:35.439
Someone guesses that
URL, they can execute it.

00:21:35.439 --> 00:21:37.230
With security controls,
what we're doing is

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:38.940
we're putting in
place IAM controls

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:41.250
using the exact
same GCP IAM that

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:45.090
let you restrict with new rules,
like an invoker function, who

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.700
is actually going to be
able to execute that.

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:49.561
Cloud SQL Direct
Connect, huge use case

00:21:49.561 --> 00:21:51.060
that we hear from
people, of course,

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.780
is I need to store data.

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:57.480
So you've been able to do
Direct Connect with Cloud SQL.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.130
This is a fully supported path.

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:01.352
And then finally, near
and dear to my heart,

00:22:01.352 --> 00:22:03.810
is how do you store those little
bits of metadata sometimes

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:05.851
that are associated with
your app, configuration,

00:22:05.851 --> 00:22:06.490
other pieces?

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:09.000
So we've introduced
environment variables.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.390
And our take is with this, with
the new languages, with the GA,

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:15.400
with these core capabilities--
and just for clarity,

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:17.699
these core capabilities
are in various states.

00:22:17.699 --> 00:22:19.240
Many of them are
coming out in alpha.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:22.170
So there's a URL where you can
sign up to get access to these.

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:25.350
All of them are
scheduled for this year.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:28.320
With this set, we really
feel like we've now

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:31.530
brought GCF to the place
where it was meant to be.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.582
We have a great GA
product that I really

00:22:33.582 --> 00:22:36.040
am excited for everyone in this
room to give a try and use.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:41.580
Now I talked about,
of course, events.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:43.770
And for the sake
of time, we're not

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:45.240
going to dive into this today.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:46.590
There's other sessions.

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:48.660
But one of the things
that I'm most excited

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:51.300
about with serverless is
the way that you can rethink

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:52.939
your application architecture.

00:22:52.939 --> 00:22:55.230
And if you're not familiar
with what we're saying here,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:57.750
really briefly, if you just
think about the canonical case

00:22:57.750 --> 00:22:58.950
of a photo upload.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:22:59.700
You get a photo.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:01.250
You need to upload it,
and you need to store it.

00:23:01.250 --> 00:23:02.520
You want to resize it.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:04.440
Does that upload come
through your app?

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:06.960
Does your app then
decide how to store it?

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.330
And then do you then
trigger some resize?

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:11.164
The nice thing about
an event trigger system

00:23:11.164 --> 00:23:13.454
is you just have the client--
whatever that client is--

00:23:13.454 --> 00:23:14.910
directly put it in GCS bucket.

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:16.770
That GCS bucket
generates a trigger.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:18.510
And then your code
gets executed.

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:20.980
And so you can go from
100, 1,000 lines of code

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:23.070
to 2, 5, 10 lines of code.

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:24.896
And of course GCS
is a great example.

00:23:24.896 --> 00:23:26.520
But maybe sometimes
we get a little bit

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.140
bored of hearing about GCS.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:30.930
So one of the things to note is
we have this incredible Pub/Sub

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:31.770
integration.

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:34.449
And so much of GCP is
integrated with Pub/Sub.

00:23:34.449 --> 00:23:36.240
There's actually over
20 different services

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.380
that you can take
advantage of today.

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:42.997
So for example, BigQuery,
ML Engine, Stackdriver.

00:23:42.997 --> 00:23:44.580
Actually, one of my
favorite use cases

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:47.040
is you can create an
alert in Stackdriver that

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:51.150
triggers a Pub/Sub event
that calls a Cloud Function.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:53.790
So for example, if
you want to, hey, I've

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:57.270
noticed we have a
failure condition where,

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:59.910
when a machine in my
data center crashes,

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:01.260
we want to trigger something.

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:01.990
You can do that.

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:03.573
And you can do that
very, very simply,

00:24:03.573 --> 00:24:05.750
and fully manage, create
the trigger in Stackdriver,

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:07.050
push it through Pub/Sub.

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:11.020
So these are all
available with GCF.

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:12.270
They're working great.

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:14.730
And we see, in fact,
huge amount of adoption

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:18.130
comes through these
products today.

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:20.350
Now even with everything
that I've outlined,

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:22.300
that's not enough, right?

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:24.390
And when we are out and
talking to customers,

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.660
what we consistently hear
are two additional problems.

00:24:28.660 --> 00:24:29.650
So one is dependencies.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:31.510
It's great that we
now support Node 8.

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:33.700
It's great that we now
support Python 3.7.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:37.270
But what happens if
I have some random--

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:42.580
or not so random-- actually,
ImageMagick or FFmpeg

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:45.740
are the canonical examples
that come up every single day.

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:47.180
I want to resize an image.

00:24:47.180 --> 00:24:49.660
I want to transcode some video.

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:52.490
And I don't like the libraries
that you've included.

00:24:52.490 --> 00:24:54.667
Now historically on a
serverless platform,

00:24:54.667 --> 00:24:55.750
the answer has been tough.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:59.080
Or you do some crazy hoops,
custom, compile, binaries,

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.290
upload it into the blob, things
I don't think anyone really

00:25:02.290 --> 00:25:03.440
wants to do.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:06.550
And of course, the other one
I alluded to earlier is people

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:08.120
say, hey, this is great.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:11.120
But how do I run these
workloads elsewhere?

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:13.420
How do I make sure that I
can have the portability I'm

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:14.900
looking for?

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:18.310
And so to address that, I want
to introduce a new concept

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:18.850
here.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:20.683
And we're going to build
off of this concept

00:25:20.683 --> 00:25:22.770
to some new products.

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.730
So the new concept is
the serverless container.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:26.146
And what's a
serverless container?

00:25:26.146 --> 00:25:28.715
It's just a name, a
descoping of container.

00:25:28.715 --> 00:25:30.340
If you're all familiar
with containers,

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:31.580
containers do many, many things.

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:32.996
But one of the key
things they are

00:25:32.996 --> 00:25:35.590
is they're just [INAUDIBLE]
file of stuff, right?

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:38.050
And if we define
a little bit more

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:39.580
what has to be in
that container,

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:41.660
it gets us wonderful things.

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.850
So in our case, with a
serverless container, what

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:47.350
we're defining them is we're
going to say they're stateless.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:49.527
Don't write to local disk
and expect it to persist.

00:25:49.527 --> 00:25:51.610
We're going to say it's a
request response, right?

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:56.760
So this isn't for long-running,
6-hour, 12-hour, 5-day jobs,

00:25:56.760 --> 00:26:00.510
but based on an event response.

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:01.520
And let's be explicit.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:03.610
HTTP is, of course,
the canonical example.

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:05.500
But events are always
an example as well.

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:06.250
They're going to auto-scale.

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:07.240
They're going have
health checks.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:09.550
So there's actually a
spec that we've published.

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:11.360
And I'll show you the URL later.

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:14.530
So if we define and scope
these containers down,

00:26:14.530 --> 00:26:16.270
one of the benefits
it gets us is we

00:26:16.270 --> 00:26:18.590
realize that this is actually
what we're doing today

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:20.590
with App Engine, and what
we're doing with Cloud

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:21.560
Functions under the hood.

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:23.380
This is when we talked about
gVisor, how we're actually

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:24.470
running things.

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:27.410
And we realized, well, why don't
we expose that to our users?

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:31.840
And so what I'm really
excited to announce

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:34.124
is serverless containers on GCF.

00:26:34.124 --> 00:26:36.290
This is something that's
coming out later this year.

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:37.220
So it's EAP now.

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:38.807
We do have customers using it.

00:26:38.807 --> 00:26:40.390
And you can see
there's a sign-up link

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:41.181
down at the bottom.

00:26:41.181 --> 00:26:42.735
Please, please sign up.

00:26:42.735 --> 00:26:44.110
And the idea here
is we are going

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:47.109
to take a container that matches
that serverless container spec.

00:26:47.109 --> 00:26:48.400
It's going to be fully managed.

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:50.300
And we're just going
to run it for you.

00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:53.230
And so what you can now
do with this is maybe

00:26:53.230 --> 00:26:54.760
you just have one
small open source

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.020
package you want to install.

00:26:56.020 --> 00:26:57.730
Maybe you have some
proprietary binaries

00:26:57.730 --> 00:26:59.830
you don't have the
source to give us to.

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:00.850
That's fine.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:03.350
Maybe you want to integrate
this with your workflows, right?

00:27:03.350 --> 00:27:04.510
People have CI/CD.

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:07.037
What we've realized is that
containers are not just

00:27:07.037 --> 00:27:09.370
the packaging format, but
they're the interchange format

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:10.880
as well.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:12.640
All of those things
are now possible.

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:15.160
And so what I want to do
next is give you a demo.

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:18.100
And so Steren Giannini has
joined me up here on stage.

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:21.710
And we're going to switch
over to the computer he has.

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:24.340
So what you can see
is pretty easy, right?

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:26.419
What we have here is
just a Dockerfile.

00:27:26.419 --> 00:27:28.210
There's literally
nothing special about it.

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:30.310
This is as boring
a Dockerfile as one

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:32.042
could ever hope to
have in their life.

00:27:32.042 --> 00:27:34.000
And you can see, though,
that in the Dockerfile

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:37.720
we just installed with apt-get
one additional package.

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:40.965
This is an open source
3D rendering package.

00:27:40.965 --> 00:27:42.340
This is not
something you've ever

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:43.964
been able to do with
serverless before.

00:27:43.964 --> 00:27:45.970
You're like, oh, I want
to 3D render some image,

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:47.428
and have it be
horizontally scaled.

00:27:47.428 --> 00:27:48.520
I want fully managed.

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:50.200
And you've been out of luck.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:52.520
Well now you just
specify it in here.

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.970
And then you build it.

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:56.240
You can build it locally.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:57.550
You can build it with
Google Cloud Build.

00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:58.133
We don't care.

00:27:58.133 --> 00:27:59.490
It's just a Docker image.

00:27:59.490 --> 00:28:00.740
We've already built it, right?

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:02.860
So we're taking advantage of
the fact that it's already up.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:04.630
It's running on our
Container Registry--

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:06.580
Google Container Registry.

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:08.280
Don't care where it
runs, particularly.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:09.500
And what Steren's
now going to do

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.041
is he's just going to hit Enter,
and he's going to deploy it.

00:28:12.041 --> 00:28:15.050
And what I want you to note is
he's using the same command--

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:16.750
gcloud functions deploy.

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:18.550
He's just added the --image.

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:19.990
If he left off the
--image, image,

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:22.289
it would just be expecting
source code and building it.

00:28:22.289 --> 00:28:23.830
But in this case,
we're like, no, no.

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:25.802
Let's give you
that Docker image.

00:28:25.802 --> 00:28:27.760
And again, like I said,
there's nothing special

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:29.440
about this Docker image.

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:32.710
This takes about 30
to 45 seconds to run.

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:33.415
It is in the AP.

00:28:33.415 --> 00:28:35.570
I promise it'll get faster.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:39.280
But now what you can see
is this is running live.

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:41.950
I mean, if anyone is able
to get that URL and copy it,

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:43.030
you'll see it yourself.

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:45.294
And you'll notice
that what we have here

00:28:45.294 --> 00:28:46.960
is we're actually
using this open source

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:50.500
package to 3D render
in not quite real time,

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:51.790
but pretty darn fast.

00:28:51.790 --> 00:28:55.240
If it's not cached, it usually
takes about half a second.

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:56.770
And it just renders some text.

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:58.790
Now we're not the best
designers in the world.

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:00.830
This doesn't look like
the most amazing thing.

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:02.500
But what we have here
is literally something

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:04.450
I don't think we've ever
been able to do before,

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:06.550
which is we've been able
to take a Cloud Function,

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:09.160
have a custom piece of
software installed into it,

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:11.170
and have that run with
all of the experience

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:13.890
that you'd be used to-- the
same commands and everything.

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:15.640
Steren's going to stay
up here and join me

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:17.040
on stage for a little bit more.

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:19.257
But we're going to
switch back to the slides

00:29:19.257 --> 00:29:20.215
for just a few seconds.

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:24.720
Because this is now
point number two.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:25.950
So remember, I said
point number one,

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:27.450
the blocker that
we hear from people

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:29.810
is that they're concerned
about the ability

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:31.920
to run arbitrary
additional dependencies.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.140
So we just showed you that.

00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:36.470
Point number two is that
he asked about consistency.

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:38.870
And one of the things
we hear is, hey,

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:41.150
you know, for whatever reason--

00:29:41.150 --> 00:29:44.412
good, bad, ugly-- we want
to run things on-prem.

00:29:44.412 --> 00:29:46.620
And there are some really
good use cases, by the way.

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:50.150
One of my favorites is
one of our big customers

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:53.240
is a oil management
oil services company.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:56.690
And they have giant oil rigs
in the middle of the ocean.

00:29:56.690 --> 00:29:59.720
And it turns out that oil rigs
don't have good bandwidth.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:03.560
And they generate terabytes
of data off of these oil rigs.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.080
And they want to be able
to process these data.

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:08.215
And so they've installed--
or are in the process

00:30:08.215 --> 00:30:08.840
of installing--

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:10.940
Kubernetes clusters
onto oil rigs,

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:13.550
because you just can't get
a terabyte of data a day

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:14.967
off of a--

00:30:14.967 --> 00:30:15.800
actually, I'm sorry.

00:30:15.800 --> 00:30:17.860
They're in petabytes a week.

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:20.510
So it's literally impossible.

00:30:20.510 --> 00:30:23.120
I guess you'd send
helicopters, right?

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:25.787
So in their case, they can do
all of this processing on site.

00:30:25.787 --> 00:30:27.370
But what they keep
on saying is, yeah,

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:29.450
but we don't have to
write different code.

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:30.327
For all of our--

00:30:30.327 --> 00:30:32.660
90% of the time when we're
running it in the cloud, this

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:33.410
is great.

00:30:33.410 --> 00:30:35.990
What do we do when we
have our on-prem needs.

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:39.170
And as you may have seen
actually in this room,

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:40.680
just before-- an hour ago--

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.890
[INAUDIBLE] were talking
about our Kubernetes story.

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:46.250
And they announced
our GKE on-prem.

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:48.770
So GKE on-prem is a
package distribution

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:51.680
that allows you to get a
fully managed installation

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:53.880
GKE running in your
own data center.

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:57.350
And this directly addresses
one of the biggest concerns

00:30:57.350 --> 00:30:58.460
that our customers have--

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:01.520
how can we have the
same environment

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:04.430
that runs in the cloud
and in our data center?

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:06.650
And so we're taking that
one step further as well

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.410
in collaboration with them.

00:31:09.410 --> 00:31:12.280
And what we're doing is we're
introducing a new add-on.

00:31:12.280 --> 00:31:16.220
So the GKE serverless add-on
takes the exact same API

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:19.610
that we expressed and
showed before, and now

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:23.150
lets you deploy these same
exact workloads onto GKE.

00:31:23.150 --> 00:31:24.470
Again, there's a sign-up link.

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:25.241
Take note of that.

00:31:25.241 --> 00:31:26.240
We can share this later.

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:28.340
But if you're interested
in participating in this,

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:30.960
please do take a look
and sign up for it.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:33.380
And the idea here is--

00:31:33.380 --> 00:31:35.120
actually, let's
show and not tell.

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:38.040
So the idea here is exactly
what we showed before.

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:39.290
So let's actually do a demo.

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:43.010
So if we're going to
go back to Steren.

00:31:43.010 --> 00:31:45.010
So here's what we're
going to do now.

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:46.420
We're going to make no changes--

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:47.830
except to the command line.

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:53.050
So Steren is going to deploy
the exact same function, right,

00:31:53.050 --> 00:31:54.490
the same image.

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:56.560
Instead of using
gcloud function deploy,

00:31:56.560 --> 00:31:57.890
we do have a new name space.

00:31:57.890 --> 00:32:00.660
So it's gcloud
serverless deploy.

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:02.500
And we need to add one
more additional piece

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:03.700
of information, of
course, because now we're

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:04.810
targeting a cluster.

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:07.060
It's not just this
world-spanning Google Cloud.

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:09.590
We need to say which specific
cluster we want to go to.

00:32:09.590 --> 00:32:12.970
So he's going to deploy
it to his very aptly named

00:32:12.970 --> 00:32:14.320
my-cluster.

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:15.927
And away we'll go.

00:32:15.927 --> 00:32:18.510
I want you to note that this is
using the same command, right?

00:32:18.510 --> 00:32:19.510
It's the gcloud command.

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:21.440
It's the same experience
we're used to.

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.650
And away, this is
going to deploy.

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:25.450
This one actually
takes about 15 seconds.

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:28.560
It's actually faster in
general to deploy to this.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:30.730
And so right now
what's going on?

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:32.950
We are spinning up a
new service in GKE.

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:35.140
So this is running in
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:37.152
And if you've managed
Kubernetes before, you

00:32:37.152 --> 00:32:39.610
know that getting a service
running, there's a lot of YAML,

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:40.870
there's a lot of configuration.

00:32:40.870 --> 00:32:42.328
And that's the
power of Kubernetes.

00:32:42.328 --> 00:32:43.960
That's why you want
it, oftentimes.

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:46.384
But as a developer
sometimes you don't, right?

00:32:46.384 --> 00:32:47.800
Sometimes you don't
need all that.

00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:50.200
And in fact, all of those
sharp edges can cut you.

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:51.699
And so what we want
to give you here

00:32:51.699 --> 00:32:53.790
is the experience of
just simply deploying.

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:55.540
And so you'll see
the URL is different.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:56.750
This is now a live demo.

00:32:56.750 --> 00:32:59.620
This is actually running
on a Kubernetes cluster.

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:02.050
And it's the exact same code.

00:33:02.050 --> 00:33:05.494
We have had to make absolutely
no changes of any kind.

00:33:05.494 --> 00:33:07.660
It's actually even worth
noting that this cluster is

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:09.970
provisioned with really
beefy big instances.

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:12.220
So we happened to be
doing this 3D computation.

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:13.970
It runs even faster.

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:16.430
And so this points to one of
the benefits that you have.

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:18.736
You are, back to that
model I talked about--

00:33:18.736 --> 00:33:20.860
this is the programming
and the operational model--

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:23.470
you are, of course, trading off
some of the operational model.

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:24.702
No, this isn't pay for usage.

00:33:24.702 --> 00:33:25.910
It's running in your cluster.

00:33:25.910 --> 00:33:27.340
You have to manage your cluster.

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:30.320
But what you do get is the
control that comes with it.

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:33.790
If you want to run 64 core
VMs, you go for it, right?

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:35.800
You can do anything
you want in this case.

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:38.440
So it gives you a
lot more flexibility.

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:40.195
Steren, thank you very much.

00:33:40.195 --> 00:33:44.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:52.570
Now it's not sufficient to
simply give you the software

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:53.890
and have it managed.

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:55.600
One of the things
we hear all the time

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:58.040
is just how important
freedom of choice is, right?

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:03.130
I was actually talking
to this chief architect

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:05.842
of a huge financial
services company, who,

00:34:05.842 --> 00:34:07.300
being a financial
services company,

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:08.949
won't let me use
their name on stage.

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:10.750
I was talking to him
a few months ago.

00:34:10.750 --> 00:34:13.449
And they have a giant
Kubernetes cluster.

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:14.219
Nothing about GKE.

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:15.718
They have a giant
Kubernetes cluster

00:34:15.718 --> 00:34:18.100
that they do for the
financial analysis right now.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:24.909
They have over 10,000 people
who they consider developers

00:34:24.909 --> 00:34:26.127
inside of their company.

00:34:26.127 --> 00:34:28.210
And they're saying, how
do I enable this developer

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.639
productivity in the clusters
that we already have?

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:33.580
And we hear this all the time,
people saying, this is great,

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.679
but we live in a
heterogeneous world.

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:37.420
It's not sufficient
that it's great

00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:39.489
that you have the
best thing on GCP.

00:34:39.489 --> 00:34:42.290
How can we use
everything at once?

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:45.400
And so I'm really excited
to bring all of this

00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:49.409
together by introducing
to you a new project.

00:34:49.409 --> 00:34:50.830
It's called Knative.

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:52.060
AUDIENCE: Whoo!

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:53.719
OREN TEICH: And
someone's excited.

00:34:53.719 --> 00:34:56.380
This is a new,
open-source project.

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:59.860
It's created by Google,
but done in conjunction

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:02.110
with a huge number of partners.

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:04.360
And the whole point
of Knative was

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:06.670
to bring the building blocks
of serverless together,

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:09.040
and to give you this
workload portability.

00:35:09.040 --> 00:35:11.082
So Knative is made
up of, like I said,

00:35:11.082 --> 00:35:12.415
it's built on top of Kubernetes.

00:35:12.415 --> 00:35:14.860
It's about the portability.

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:17.330
And it's key primitives.

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:19.870
So specifically today,
Knative has three primitives

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:21.200
built into it.

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:22.460
It has build.

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:25.112
So what you can do with
build is take source code

00:35:25.112 --> 00:35:26.320
and actually get a container.

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:27.739
Because by the
way, even though it

00:35:27.739 --> 00:35:29.530
was easy to build a
container, I don't want

00:35:29.530 --> 00:35:31.360
to deal with containers myself.

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:32.530
So we build that.

00:35:32.530 --> 00:35:33.557
Serving, right?

00:35:33.557 --> 00:35:35.140
That piece that
Steren just showed you

00:35:35.140 --> 00:35:37.360
where he types gcloud
serverless deploy?

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:38.570
That's what serving does.

00:35:38.570 --> 00:35:40.528
There's nothing you need
to worry about, right?

00:35:40.528 --> 00:35:42.410
There's none of the
configuration pieces.

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:44.290
And then we're not even
demoing this today,

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:45.580
but event binding, right?

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:48.970
How do you have a standard
mechanism of binding to events,

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:51.070
and making this available?

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:54.130
Now if we just did
this by ourselves,

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:55.690
I think that'd be interesting.

00:35:55.690 --> 00:36:06.660
But ultimately our goal is
to bring the serverless world

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:08.070
together around this.

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:09.900
And so from that
perspective we've

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:12.180
been working with a
large number of partners

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:15.730
for many, many months on this--

00:36:15.730 --> 00:36:20.040
SAP, IBM Pivotal, Red Hat,
and many, many others.

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:22.470
And in fact, if you search
Twitter for Knative today,

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:26.790
you'll see a pretty remarkable
stream of blog posts

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:29.730
of announcements, of companies
announcing the strategies

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:31.950
behind this.

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:34.530
Our hope, our
aspiration, our goal

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:37.890
here is to enable a whole
flourishing ecosystem

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:39.930
of serverless
products to come out,

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:43.070
but to enable those in a
compatible, portable way

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:45.330
so that as a developer
you can take advantage

00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:47.730
of the great
serverless concepts,

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:50.010
but also take advantage
of the specific pieces

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:52.670
that each company
is going to provide.

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:55.757
So to that point, I'd love to
bring up one of our partners.

00:36:55.757 --> 00:36:57.590
So Michael, if you
wouldn't mind joining me,

00:36:57.590 --> 00:37:00.050
Michael Wintergerst is from SAP.

00:37:00.050 --> 00:37:01.310
Come on up.

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:03.650
And we've been
working with SAP now

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:06.110
for quite some time on this.

00:37:06.110 --> 00:37:08.360
In fact, the whole front
row is SAP, by the way,

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:10.234
and I suspect there's
more of them out there.

00:37:10.234 --> 00:37:12.380
So we're all very excited.

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:13.537
Michael, thank you.

00:37:13.537 --> 00:37:14.537
MICHAEL WINTERGERST: Hi.

00:37:14.537 --> 00:37:18.457
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:18.457 --> 00:37:20.290
OREN TEICH: So Michael,
tell me a little bit

00:37:20.290 --> 00:37:22.990
about why SAP was
interested in Knative,

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:24.330
and what got you up here?

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:24.580
MICHAEL WINTERGERST: Yeah.

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:25.510
So, Knative.

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:28.310
And I'm responsible for
the SAP Cloud Platform.

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:31.270
So that is our enterprise
platform as a service offering.

00:37:31.270 --> 00:37:33.650
So it's all around
open source frameworks.

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:35.620
We have, for
example, Kubernetes.

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:37.430
We have Cloud Foundry in.

00:37:37.430 --> 00:37:40.720
And Knative and
serverless computing

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:42.850
brings us the
possibility to ease

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:46.150
the whole development of our
business services and business

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:47.530
applications.

00:37:47.530 --> 00:37:50.320
For instance, our developers
are telling us, hey, it's

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:52.180
great that you have all
the nice frameworks.

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:53.380
But here's my code.

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:54.400
Just run it, right?

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:57.400
So that is a paradigm our
developers have in mind.

00:37:57.400 --> 00:37:59.650
And therefore,
serverless, with the idea

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:02.890
to hide all the complexity
of server infrastructure,

00:38:02.890 --> 00:38:07.030
networking storage, pay per
use auto-scaling, that's

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:09.250
really great, and brings
a lot of capabilities

00:38:09.250 --> 00:38:10.870
to our application folks.

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:14.860
And with Knative, you're
adding a lot more capabilities.

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:17.590
As you said already
before, here's my code.

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:19.580
Create a Docker image out of it.

00:38:19.580 --> 00:38:22.750
Register that in
a Docker registry.

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:24.600
Deploy it on Kubernetes.

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:27.880
And also bringing then the
inventing stuff inside.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:30.520
That is key for our
customers and partners

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:32.080
in order to create
business services

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:34.120
and enterprise-grade
applications.

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:35.890
OREN TEICH: And you're
not just looking

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:37.914
at using it as a
integrational piece,

00:38:37.914 --> 00:38:40.330
but you're actually building
your own product on top of it

00:38:40.330 --> 00:38:41.050
as well, right?

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:42.258
MICHAEL WINTERGERST: Exactly.

00:38:42.258 --> 00:38:44.800
And I'm super excited
today that we announce

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:47.570
in the morning, the first
open source Lighthouse project

00:38:47.570 --> 00:38:49.940
on Knative, our project Kyma.

00:38:49.940 --> 00:38:54.550
So kudos to our C4/HANA guys
sitting here in the front.

00:38:54.550 --> 00:38:55.684
So they made that happen.

00:38:55.684 --> 00:38:57.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:57.920 --> 00:38:59.220
So what is Kyma all about?

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:00.480
And please have a look.

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:04.270
It's on GitHub--
github/kyma/project.io.

00:39:07.750 --> 00:39:11.860
So it's an extension factory
for our digital core.

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:17.380
So maybe you know at SAP
we're adopting a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:17.380 --> 00:39:19.240
So that's nothing
what we have invented,

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:21.670
but it's coming from
Gartner and IDC.

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:23.390
They shaped theirs
two years ago.

00:39:23.390 --> 00:39:24.790
So we have our digital core.

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:28.870
So our big enterprise resource
planning systems we have.

00:39:28.870 --> 00:39:31.024
That's our mode one environment.

00:39:31.024 --> 00:39:32.440
So where the
customers are running

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:33.650
their day-to-day business.

00:39:33.650 --> 00:39:35.150
But on the other
hand, our customers

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:37.750
would also like to see, hey, I
would like to get innovations,

00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:40.660
like IoT, blockchain,
machine learning,

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:43.930
all of those capabilities also
in the mode one environment.

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:45.409
And therefore, we
said at SAP, it

00:39:45.409 --> 00:39:47.200
makes sense to have an
innovation platform,

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:49.570
our so-called SAP
Cloud Platform where

00:39:49.570 --> 00:39:52.180
we are running the complete
innovation stack on top.

00:39:52.180 --> 00:39:55.690
And Kyma is sitting on top
of the SAP Cloud Platform,

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:58.570
and it's the glue code behind
the mode one and the mode two

00:39:58.570 --> 00:40:02.020
environment, allowing you to
create extensions to your mode

00:40:02.020 --> 00:40:05.660
one environment, so that you
can run all the nice machine

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:08.680
learning features next
to your mode one system

00:40:08.680 --> 00:40:09.850
without disrupting them.

00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:11.190
So that is our approach.

00:40:11.190 --> 00:40:12.940
OREN TEICH: And then
I hear from customers

00:40:12.940 --> 00:40:15.524
all the time that
one of the, I think,

00:40:15.524 --> 00:40:17.440
the confusions that comes
up around serverless

00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:19.440
is they think it's just
about greenfield, right?

00:40:19.440 --> 00:40:21.650
And they're just going,
but I can't rip and replace

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:22.150
everything.

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:23.582
I have an SAP system.

00:40:23.582 --> 00:40:24.790
MICHAEL WINTERGERST: Exactly.

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:25.510
OREN TEICH: And of
course we're not

00:40:25.510 --> 00:40:27.370
going to replace your
SAP existing ERP.

00:40:27.370 --> 00:40:29.840
There's no world in which
anyone would want to do that.

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:31.000
But how do I extend it?

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:32.832
How do I do the new
development, right?

00:40:32.832 --> 00:40:34.540
And I think that's
what Kyma's all about.

00:40:34.540 --> 00:40:35.020
MICHAEL WINTERGERST: Exactly.

00:40:35.020 --> 00:40:38.030
So a nice example is when you
have, for example, a commerce

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:41.260
system coming from SAP,
and an order gets created,

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:43.877
or a new shopping
cart gets created,

00:40:43.877 --> 00:40:45.460
you would like to
create an extension.

00:40:45.460 --> 00:40:48.390
So you would like to
get functions, which

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:50.080
tightly connect to this event.

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:52.750
All-in-all it gets created
in our mode one environment.

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:55.250
But behind, you have the
machine-learning capabilities.

00:40:55.250 --> 00:40:57.190
And you can bring in
those capabilities

00:40:57.190 --> 00:40:59.200
without changing your
mode one environment.

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:01.390
And that is the key
benefit we are getting out

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:02.872
of Knative and Kyma.

00:41:02.872 --> 00:41:03.580
OREN TEICH: Yeah.

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:06.320
I got to say, it's literally
what every customer I know

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:07.180
asks for.

00:41:07.180 --> 00:41:09.640
And I'm just so excited that
you were able to join us,

00:41:09.640 --> 00:41:10.820
and that you're
able to participate.

00:41:10.820 --> 00:41:11.270
Thank you.

00:41:11.270 --> 00:41:12.000
MICHAEL WINTERGERST: Thank you.

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:12.692
Thanks, Oren.

00:41:12.692 --> 00:41:16.136
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:22.620
OREN TEICH: So I would strongly
encourage you to try Knative.

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:25.480
It's early days.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.130
There's lots of
people building on it.

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:29.320
In case you're
wondering, Knative is not

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:30.650
designed for the end user.

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:32.410
It's designed to be
the infrastructure

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:36.400
pieces that enable products
to be built on top of it.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.510
Kyma was built on top of it.

00:41:38.510 --> 00:41:40.210
We've built our
Kubernetes add-on--

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:41.674
serverless add-on--
on top of it.

00:41:41.674 --> 00:41:43.090
But if you'd like
to get involved,

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:44.710
if you'd like to see what
the open source is looking

00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:46.626
like, if you want to see
how we're doing scale

00:41:46.626 --> 00:41:48.880
to zero on a Kubernetes
cluster, go take a look

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:51.500
and see what we're doing.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.890
So this is the last slide today.

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:56.500
And what I wanted to do is
give you a quick overview

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:57.820
of what we talked about--

00:41:57.820 --> 00:42:00.490
including some of the
things we didn't talk about.

00:42:00.490 --> 00:42:03.070
So when we talk about
serverless right now,

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:04.900
of course there's
App Engine, right?

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:07.690
And in a recap, with
App Engine it's faster.

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:08.630
It's open.

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:11.264
We have the popular languages
with more coming constantly.

00:42:11.264 --> 00:42:13.180
And I didn't even have
a chance to talk about,

00:42:13.180 --> 00:42:14.830
by the way, it's
HIPAA compliant.

00:42:14.830 --> 00:42:17.500
It's been HIPAA compliant now
just as of a few months ago.

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:18.850
And so we're signing BAAs.

00:42:18.850 --> 00:42:20.350
We're seeing more
and more customers

00:42:20.350 --> 00:42:22.120
do incredible things with it.

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:26.050
On Cloud Functions, we
have, of course, is GA--

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:29.500
new regions, the new languages,
and these capabilities.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:33.190
I didn't even talk about
scaling controls, by the way.

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:34.780
One of the things
that comes up quite

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:37.354
often is you can have
a Cloud Function,

00:42:37.354 --> 00:42:38.770
and you're doing
resource pooling.

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:43.510
You don't want to have
one function starve

00:42:43.510 --> 00:42:45.467
the database if it gets
scaled up really high.

00:42:45.467 --> 00:42:47.050
So how do you actually
set some limits

00:42:47.050 --> 00:42:48.640
around what this looks like.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:50.680
And what you can
see is we're moving

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.860
from the basic
enablement capabilities

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:55.420
to what does it
really look like when

00:42:55.420 --> 00:42:58.030
I have 500 functions,
50 functions, when

00:42:58.030 --> 00:43:00.040
I have complex systems?

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:02.710
We talked about serverless
containers, and the ways

00:43:02.710 --> 00:43:07.980
that we're expressing that
through GCF coming soon

00:43:07.980 --> 00:43:11.990
through the serverless
add-on, and through Knative.

00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:15.190
And I didn't even have a chance
to talk about the green gear.

00:43:15.190 --> 00:43:17.690
But I'm going to give
it a few brief moments.

00:43:17.690 --> 00:43:19.400
I just want to call
out that we're also

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:22.430
taking a lot of the services
that have been built into App

00:43:22.430 --> 00:43:24.260
Engine historically,
and we're making

00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:26.750
them first-class citizens
within all of cloud.

00:43:26.750 --> 00:43:29.180
So scheduling,
[INAUDIBLE] in the cloud.

00:43:29.180 --> 00:43:30.830
It's like, how boring is that?

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:33.174
It turns out it's actually
really, really important.

00:43:33.174 --> 00:43:35.840
I was just talking to a customer
two days ago who was just like,

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:38.740
I just want to run something
once every 24 hours.

00:43:38.740 --> 00:43:40.370
Help?

00:43:40.370 --> 00:43:41.560
It's standard use case.

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:44.800
Maybe it's every five
minutes, every 24 hours.

00:43:44.800 --> 00:43:46.550
So Cloud Scheduler
enables you to do that.

00:43:46.550 --> 00:43:48.140
And that's coming
out later this year.

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:49.400
Cloud Tasks.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:52.010
Task Queues was a built-in
system into App Engine.

00:43:52.010 --> 00:43:53.854
Cloud Tasks is a
mechanism of doing

00:43:53.854 --> 00:43:55.020
inter-process communication.

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:57.260
So if you want to fire
off one thing to another,

00:43:57.260 --> 00:43:59.390
and you need a place to
store them in between,

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:00.720
that's what Cloud Tasks does.

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:02.660
And I already mentioned
Cloud Firestore.

00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:05.525
So all of these make up what
we think of as serverless.

00:44:05.525 --> 00:44:06.910
And they're just the first step.

00:44:06.910 --> 00:44:09.200
And we're really excited about
what you might be building,

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:10.241
where you might be going.

00:44:10.241 --> 00:44:12.367
And I hope that you're
excited about this,

00:44:12.367 --> 00:44:14.450
that you're going to go
to some of other sessions,

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:16.366
and that you're going
to try our products out.

00:44:16.366 --> 00:44:17.540
So thank you so much.

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:20.240
[APPLAUSE]

